/// Chapter 20 of the guide, written from md-docs/about/accessibility.md by
/// scripts/docsite/guide.py; edit the guide, not this file.
page AboutAccessibility(path: "/about/accessibility", title: "Accessibility", description: "What the built-ins do for keyboard and screen-reader users, the A01–A18 checks, and the part of accessibility that stays a person's judgement.", layout: DocsShell("Accessibility", kind: .explanation, chapter: t("nav.group.about"), blurb: "Pages that work with a keyboard, a screen reader or voice control: what the built-ins do, what the compiler checks, and what no tool can check for you.", source: "md-docs/about/accessibility.md", sections: [Anchor(id: "what-the-built-ins-do", label: "What the built-ins do"), Anchor(id: "what-the-compiler-checks", label: "What the compiler checks"), Anchor(id: "what-the-compiler-cannot-check", label: "What the compiler cannot check")], prevTo: "/about/how-reactivity-works", prevLabel: t("ch.19"), nextTo: "/about/security", nextLabel: t("ch.21"))) {
    Stack(gap: .lg) {
        Markdown("This essay is about the accessibility a WebFluent page has before you do anything, the part the compiler checks on every build, and the part that stays yours: building pages people can use with a keyboard, a screen reader, voice control, zoom or reduced motion.\n\nMost inaccessible pages were not made that way on purpose. A `<div>` with a click handler looks like a button and is not one; a field whose label is a placeholder looks labelled until the reader types. Each is a small mistake that a checklist would catch, and checklists are read once.\n\nWebFluent moves as much of the checklist as it can out of your hands. The built-ins render the markup assistive technology expects, the router and forms manage focus, and the compiler reports what it can see as a finding with a code, on every build. That leaves the work no tool can do (meaning, wording, order) and frees your attention for it.")

        Heading("What the built-ins do", id: "what-the-built-ins-do").h2

        Markdown("You get these behaviours without asking, as long as you use the built-in elements rather than rebuilding them:\n\n- **Semantic elements.** A `Button` is a `<button>`, a `Link` an `<a>`, `Heading(…).h2` an `<h2>`. `Header` and `Footer` are landmarks, and so is a `Section` given an `aria-label`. A `Table` is a `<table>` whose head cells are `<th scope=\"col\">`, a `List` is a `<ul>` or `<ol>`, and the page's content is a `<main>`.\n- **A skip link.** It is first in the tab order, visible when focused, and jumps past the navigation to the content.\n- **Route changes that behave like page loads.** Focus moves to the new page's heading, the scroll returns to the top, and the new title is announced.\n- **Labelled fields.** `label:` is a real `<label for>`. `hint:` and the error are tied to the control with `aria-describedby`. An error sets `aria-invalid` and is announced when it appears ([Forms](/learn/14-forms)).\n- **Forms that point at the problem.** A submit that fails moves focus to the first field with an error and announces it.\n- **Dialogs that hold focus.** `Modal` and `Dialog` are real `<dialog>` elements: the rest of the page is inert, Escape closes them, and focus returns to what opened them.\n- **Focus held where you ask.** Any other element holds focus with `trapFocus: open` — a find bar, a panel. Focus moves in, Tab and Shift+Tab go round inside it, a click outside brings focus back, and when `open` turns false, focus returns to where it was.\n- **Menus with the keys people expect.** The arrow keys move and skip a disabled item, Home and End jump to the ends, Enter and Space choose, ArrowRight opens a submenu and ArrowLeft leaves it, and Escape closes the innermost menu and returns focus. A check item is a `menuitemcheckbox` with `aria-checked`; an item's shortcut is its `aria-keyshortcuts`.\n- **Splitters you can move without a mouse.** A `Splitter` is a `separator` with its size as `aria-valuenow`. It takes focus, the arrow keys move it, Home and End go to its ends, and Enter resets it.\n- **Announcements.** A `Toast` is spoken politely. To announce anything else, call `WF.announce(text)`.\n- **The active link.** The link to the current page carries `aria-current=\"page\"`.\n- **Reduced motion.** A reader who asked for it gets no animation at all, and a carousel does not play on its own ([Motion](/recipes/motion-defaults#respect-reduced-motion)).\n- **Visible focus.** Every interactive built-in shows a focus ring for the keyboard (`:focus-visible`), but not on every click.")

        Heading("What the compiler checks", id: "what-the-compiler-checks").h2

        Markdown("The compiler checks every page for the mistakes it can see in the markup, and reports each as a warning on every build and in the editor. Each code has a full entry, with a fix, in [Diagnostics](/errors#accessibility), and `wf explain CODE` prints it in your terminal.")

        Table(caption: "What the compiler checks") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Code").header
                    Table.Cell("What it checks").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`A01`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Every `Image` has `alt:` (an empty one for a decorative picture)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`A02`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Every `IconButton` has a `label:`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`A03`, `A04`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Every `Input` has a label or at least a placeholder (prefer a label); every `Checkbox`, `Radio`, `Switch`, `Slider` and `Textarea` has a label") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`A05`, `A06`, `A07`") }
                    Table.Cell("Buttons, links and headings have text")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`A08`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Every `Modal`, `Dialog`, `AlertDialog` and `Sheet` has a `title:` (a `Sheet` may have a `label:`)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`A09`") }
                    Table.Cell("A video has captions and controls; audio has a transcript")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`A10`") }
                    Table.Cell("A table has a header row")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`A11`, `A12`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("No heading level is skipped, and each page has exactly one `h1`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`A13`") }
                    Table.Cell("The theme's text and background colours meet WCAG AA contrast")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`A14`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("A `role:` holds children of the roles it requires") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`A15`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("A control's `aria-label` contains its visible text, for voice control") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`A16`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("No literal `id:` is shared by several elements, in a loop or a reused component") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`A17`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Every `Frame` has a `title:`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`A18`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Every `Popover` drawn as a dialog has a `label:`") }
                }
            }
        }

        Markdown("For example, this button's `aria-label` does not contain the word it shows, so a voice-control user who says \"click Save\" may not reach it:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Editor(path: "/", title: "Editor", description: "Write a note.") {
    Heading("Editor").h1
    Button("Save", aria-label: "Submit the form")
}"#, title: "wf · draws A15")

        Markdown("The fix is to start the `aria-label` with the visible words: `aria-label: \"Save the note\"`.")

        Heading("What the compiler cannot check", id: "what-the-compiler-cannot-check").h2

        Markdown("The compiler can see markup; it cannot see meaning. These are still yours:\n\n- **Alt text that says what the picture shows**, in its context. \"Photo\" passes `A01` and helps nobody.\n- **Link text that says where it goes.** Twelve \"Read more\" links pass `A06`.\n- **An order that makes sense.** The reading order is the source order. Do not rearrange it visually with `order:` or absolute positioning.\n- **Colour that is not the only signal.** An error in red also says so in words; a chart has labels.\n- **The contrast of your own styles.** `A13` checks the theme's tokens, not a colour written directly in a `style \{ \}` block.\n- **Custom controls.** A `Card` made clickable with `on click` is not a button to a keyboard until it has a role, a place in the tab order and key handlers.\n- **Time limits.** A `Toast` disappears. Anything the reader must act on belongs in an `Alert` that stays.\n\nEach of these is a judgement about what a page means to the person using it, which is why no build can make it. What a tool can do is make the judgement cheaper: [Make a page work with a keyboard and a screen reader](/recipes/keyboard-and-screen-reader) names every control so it matches what a sighted reader sees, turns a card into a control a keyboard can reach, and then checks the page the way its readers use it — with only a keyboard, a screen reader, 200% zoom and reduced motion — ending with a checklist. Because `wf test` finds a control by the name a reader sees, as assistive technology does, a test that clicks through a page is also a test that someone can operate it.")
    }
}
